# max_subarray

The first line contains n (1 <= n <= 200000). The second line contains n
integers a_1 .. a_n, each with |a_i| <= 10^9.

Print the largest possible sum of a non-empty contiguous subarray, followed by
a newline.

Notes: the subarray must contain at least one element, so if every number is
negative the answer is the largest (least negative) number. The answer can be
as large as 2 * 10^14, which does not fit in a 32-bit int.

Required time complexity: O(n). Time limit: 1 second.

Sample input:

    9
    -2 1 -3 4 -1 2 1 -5 4

Sample output:

    6


Student code:
  1| #include <stdio.h>
  2| #include <limits.h>
  3| 
  4| int maxSum(int nums[], int len) {
  5|     int best = INT_MIN, here = 0;
  6| 
  7|     // Iterate through the array
  8|     for (int i = 0; i < len; i++) {
  9|         // Add current element to here
 10|         here += nums[i];
 11| 
 12|         // Update best if needed
 13|         if (best < here) {
 14|             best = here;
 15|         }
 16| 
 17|         // If here becomes negative, reset it to 0
 18|         if (here < 0) {
 19|             here = 0;
 20|         }
 21|     }
 22| 
 23|     return best;
 24| }
 25| 
 26| int main() {
 27|     int len;
 28|     scanf("%d", &len);
 29| 
 30|     // Read the array elements
 31|     int nums[len];
 32|     for (int i = 0; i < len; i++) {
 33|         scanf("%d", &nums[i]);
 34|     }
 35| 
 36|     // Find and print the maximum subarray sum
 37|     printf("%d\n", maxSum(nums, len));
 38| 
 39|     return 0;
 40| }

